    "id": "deploy.L1024.20",
    "class": "equivalent",
    "justification": "The `raise ValueError()` at module_utils/connectome_deploy.py:1024 runs only when an element of data['entries'] is not a dict. `data` comes from one place only, json.loads(self.manifest_path.read_text()) at connectome_deploy.py:1018 (_load_ownership takes no argument and line 1020 has already required 'entries' to be a list), so such an element is a JSON list, string, number, boolean or null. With the raise replaced by `pass` the very next statement, `path = Path(entry['path'])` at connectome_deploy.py:1025, subscripts that element with the string 'path', which raises TypeError for every one of those types (list and str indices must be integers; int, float, bool and None are not subscriptable). Nothing is changed in between: self.owned is first assigned at connectome_deploy.py:1037. ValueError and TypeError are caught by the same handler at connectome_deploy.py:1038 and replaced by the same DeployError('invalid ownership manifest; operator recovery required') `from None` at connectome_deploy.py:1039. Tried before waiving: manifests whose entries hold 'text', '', [], ['path'], [1,2], 7, 0, 1.5, true, false and null, alone and before/after valid entries, through prepare() and activate() on the original and on the mutant: class, message, code, cause and the number of entries loaded so far were the same in all 66 cases.",
    "no_observable_difference": "For every manifest file content: a non-dict entry gives DeployError('invalid ownership manifest; operator recovery required'), code E_DEPLOY_FAILED, __cause__ None, with the same partially filled self.owned, on both versions; manifests without a non-dict entry never reach line 1024. The one difference is the suppressed (`from None`) __context__ attribute of that DeployError (ValueError on the original, TypeError on the mutant), which no caller, boundary or diagnostic reads.",
